# Context Engineering
	- Discipline of desiging everything around the model
	- Optimizing the utility of tokens.
	- Good prompt engineering + tools +dynamic study +cost
	- Context is like RAM
![alt text](image.png)
    


## Effective context Engineering

### Write - Give your agent an external notebook

a. Giving agent ways to persist info
b. Make agent take notes -> eg. scratchpads, working note during task
c. Rules files (eg. claude.md)
d. Memory - facts and preferences persisted across sessions(like permissions). File based system that is external
c. Writing is great, but agents needs to learn to pull the right information at right time


### Select - agent selects right information at the right time. Pull in only whats relevant for the current step

a. But who makes that decision on what to select -- cant select everything
b. Eg. in Traditional RAG - system makes this. Model has no say, its one shot
c. However, in agentic RAG that changes. Agent decides what to search + when to stop
![alt text](image-2.png)
c. So, what does the agent actually select on? Thats when memory needs to be handled

 Handling Memory - Agent can pull from these types of memory. However, have to be methodical about tools.
![alt text](image-3.png)
d. Tools - Better tool selection
    - You cannot give agent 40 tools to work with. Thats too much
    - Rather, you can use RAG and setup select tool calls like below
e. This alone can push the cost down and increase accuracy by huge margin
![alt text](image-4.png)
f. LOAD some CONTEXT ahead of time but then let agent do Just In Time tool calling as needed so context is not over bloated
g. Even if you optimize good initial load, context can still build up over time. Thats when you apply compress strategies

### Compress  -  Reduce token count while preserving what matters

#### Before Agent starts working

a.Compress context before it enters - eg. RAG chunking and reranking to send only required context to LLM
![alt text](image-6.png)

b. You can also sumarie the tool outputs on the fly so main context only gets the summary.

#### While the agent is working

a. Summary each conversation after you have them. So you always have compact version.
Maybe you keep last 10 messages verbatim but keep everything else summarized.

![alt text](image-7.png)

#### After the agent is done working

a. Remove all excess tokens from LLM
![alt text](image-8.png)

### Isolate

1. Once you are done with research, there is no need for that context to linger around for implementation phase
2. For implementation, the agent should start fresh with fresh context.
3. Thats where subagents come in.
![alt text](image-9.png)

## What goes wrong when context engineering is NOT done well

### Context Poisoning and Pruning

1. A wrong information got into LLM and it keeps cascading that down. This could be wrong result from web search or wrong result from Tool
2. What is the fix?? CONTEXT PRUNING
3. Context Pruning is pruning outdated info, validate tool calls and compress error-recovery sequences.
![alt text](image-10.png)

### Context Distraction

1. When agent gets distracted with details it found and stops thinking.
2. Best solution is to keep summarizing and keep pruning
![alt text](image-11.png)

### Context Confusion

1. When superfulous contents get into the context
2. Wrong tools called for example - if you give all 46 tools, it picked wrong one. 
3. Fix - dynamic tool management. Keep tool focused on current task. Use semantic kernel and RAG based mcp approach to only pull tools that are necessary.

### Context Clash

1. Agent grabbed information that conflicts with what is already in its context.
2. Eg system prompt said one thing, retrieved doc says another, user said something else.
3. Fix - use authority ordering.
4. Validate the information from the tools before injecting into the context as much as possible.
